** Jump shifts over a minor, and opener's rebid after any jump shift (batch 8)

`responses-major.bml` has the booklet's jump shifts over a major (17+, forcing
to game); `responses-minor.bml` left them out over a minor. They are the same
call: 17+ with a good five-card suit and no fit for opener's minor. Opener then
describes his hand naturally at the cheapest level: he raises with three-card
support, bids a four-card new suit, rebids a six-card suit, and with a
balanced hand bids notrump. The jump shift forces to game, so nothing stops
below it: responder's further bidding toward game or slam is the partnership's
judgment (natural inference), and so is the auction after their interference.
`#ANYORDER`: the jump shift may be into a lower suit (1!h-3!c).

